-- title:
--   Evaluation at a transcendental element is a covering off finitely many points
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure which is a curve over $\mathbb{C}$ in the project's sense, i.e. every nonzero $g \in F$ admits a divisor $D$ with $D(v) = v.\mathrm{ord}\,g$ for all places $v$ and $\deg D = 0$, each place has residue field finite over $\mathbb{C}$, and $\Omega_{F/\mathbb{C}}$ is free of rank one over $F$; assume moreover $F$ is essentially of finite type over $\mathbb{C}$. Here a place is a valuation subring of $F$ containing $\mathbb{C}$, distinct from $F$ itself and a principal ideal ring, $v.\mathrm{ord}$ is minus the logarithm of its associated $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation, and $\mathrm{evalAt}\,v\,g$ is the image of the residue class of $g$ under a chosen left inverse of $\mathbb{C} \to \kappa(v)$ when $g$ lies in the valuation subring, and $0$ otherwise. Suppose the set $\mathrm{Place}\,\mathbb{C}\,F$ carries a topology and $\mathbb{C}$-charts making it a compact, Hausdorff, connected analytic manifold, and that the charts are compatible with the field in the following sense (hypothesis `hF`): for every nonzero $g \in F$ and every place $v$, the function $z \mapsto \mathrm{evalAt}\,((\mathrm{extChartAt}\ v)^{-1}z)\,g$ is meromorphic at the chart image of $v$ with meromorphic order exactly $v.\mathrm{ord}\,g$. Let $f \in F$ be transcendental over $\mathbb{C}$ with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $\mathbb{C}(f)$ generated by $f$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a finite set $B \subset \mathbb{C}$ such that the map sending a place $P$ with $f \in P$ to $\mathrm{evalAt}\,P\,f$ is a covering map over the complement of $B$, and such that for every $t \notin B$ the number of places $P$ with $f \in P$ and $\mathrm{evalAt}\,P\,f = t$ equals $[F : \mathbb{C}(f)]$.
--
--   This is the statement that a non-constant function on a compact Riemann surface presents it as a branched covering of the affine line, unbranched away from a finite set of critical values, with all unramified fibres of cardinality equal to the degree $[F:\mathbb{C}(f)]$. It is used in the construction of dissection scale data, where a dissection of the base is pulled back along this covering.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_isCoveringMapOn_evalAt.lean

